## Step 5 — Stone Selection

Stone selection is the last step, not the first. By the time you reach here, you know your budget tier, your occupation fit, your metal, and your setting. The stone grade fills the remaining budget.

### The Face-Up Budget Floor

This is the most important concept in this guide. Every budget tier has a stone size that makes visual sense on a men's hand. Buying a stone below that floor is the most common regret.

A 6.5mm stone (1ct) is correct on most men's hands. At 7.4mm (1.5ct) you get a noticeable step up. At 8.1mm (2ct) the ring starts to read as a statement. At 9.4mm (3ct) it reads as intentional luxury.

The mistake: buying a 0.5ct (5.2mm) stone in a heavy setting. At 5.2mm on a size 10 hand, the stone disappears inside the metal. The floor for a [men's 1 carat diamond ring](/mens-1-carat-diamond-ring) category is not arbitrary — 1ct at 6.5mm is the minimum that reads clearly on adult male hands.

| Stone Size | Face-Up Diameter | Correct For | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0.75ct | 5.9mm | Small hands (size 6–7) only | Too small on most men's hands |
| 1ct | 6.5mm | Standard — size 7–12 | The correct entry point for men's diamond rings |
| 1.5ct | 7.4mm | Standard — size 8–12 | Visible upgrade; lab 1.5ct D-VVS1 at $1,950 |
| 2ct | 8.1mm | Statement — size 9–13 | Lab 2ct D-VVS1 at $2,810; natural 2ct starts $16,490 |
| 3ct | 9.4mm | Statement — size 9–14 | Lab 3ct only at this budget; natural starts $44,500 |

**Verdict:** Match the stone size to your hand size, then pick the grade. A 1ct in white gold on a size 11 hand is correct. A 1.5ct lab on a size 9 hand at $1,950 is a better value move than 1ct natural at $3,230.

### Natural Diamond Grade Floors (1ct)

For natural GIA stones at 1ct, the grade floor is G-VS2 Excellent cut. Here is what that means in practice:

- **G color at 1ct** is colorless in white gold and platinum. No visible warmth. Correct floor.
- **VS2 clarity at 1ct** is eye-clean. No inclusion visible at normal viewing distance. Correct floor.
- **Excellent cut** is non-negotiable. Very Good cut produces dark spots at 6.5mm face-up.

Going below G-VS2 on a 1ct natural round is not a value trade — it is a visual quality loss. Going above F-VS1 is a grade-record trade, not a visual upgrade.

| Grade | Price | Cert | What this means |
|---|---|---|---|
| [GIA G-VS2 1ct $3,230](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29090690?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | $3,230 | GIA | The correct entry — G-VS2 Excellent is eye-clean and colorless in WG |
| [GIA G-VS2 1ct $3,240](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29090694?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | $3,240 | GIA | Same grade, different stone ID — verify cert at gia.edu before buying |
| [GIA G-VS1 1ct $3,400](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29203762?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | $3,400 | GIA | One clarity step above VS2 — $170 more for a grade record, not visual gain |
| [GIA E-VS2 1ct $3,540](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27878143?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | $3,540 | GIA | E color step-up — $310 more than G-VS2; correct if D/E matters to you |

**Verdict:** G-VS2 Excellent GIA at $3,230 is the right buy for most men. VS1 and E color add cost, not visible quality. Only upgrade if the grade record matters to you personally.

> "When a client asks me what stone to buy for a first men's diamond ring, I say GIA G-VS2 Excellent, every time. It is correct. It is eye-clean. It is colorless in white metal. The difference between G and E at 1ct is a grade on paper — not what you see on your hand. Spend the difference on a better setting or a higher total carat size."
> — Farzana Hasan, diamond expert | [About Farzana](/about-farzana/)

### Lab Stone Grade Floors by Size

For lab stones, the grade floor moves up as stone size increases. Inclusions and color become more visible at larger face-up diameters.

| Size | Minimum Grade | Cert | Current Price | What this means |
